WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.760
Thank you to NordVPN for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.120
Welp, it’s 2020!

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:12.500
Now be honest, how many of you decided to
kick off the new decade by making a resolution

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:13.860
to get into shape?

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.269
Does this sound familiar?

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:23.040
You head into your local gym, the bodybuilders
are pumpin’, the club music’s bumping,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.920
the smoothie station is... blending.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.980
And a sales rep presents you with a “menu”
of contract options:

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.990
Yo, you ready to get jacked?

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:32.990
Sweet.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.040
You can sign up for a whole year for $600.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.940
Or on a month-to-month basis for $58 a month.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.940
OR pay-per-visit for $10 a day.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.240
So… which one should I choose?

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:57.400
The “New You” plans on working out 3 times
a week, so the cost-per-visit for each plan

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.160
shakes out like this. We're not including
those extra “startup” fees, since most

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.810
of the time, those only exist so they can
be waived, to make you feel like you’re

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:07.810
getting a bargain.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:12.170
From a purely mathematical standpoint, the
annual contract makes the most sense.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.950
And, maybe by “locking yourself in,” you’ll
be more likely to stick to your resolution.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.770
Psychologists call this strategy “precommitment,”
when your present self tries to control the

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.890
behavior of your future self by making obligations
that your future self can’t get out of.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:32.580
Social scientist Jon Elster cited the story
of Odysseus and the Sirens as a classic example

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.780
of precommitment.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.040
The Sirens were creatures of Greek mythology
whose beautiful songs lured sailors to wreck

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.990
their ships on the rocks.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.890
Odysseus wanted to hear the Sirens’ songs,
so he instructed his men to tie him to the

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:46.890
mast.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.920
In other words, he didn’t trust his future
self to resist temptation, so he took steps

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.440
to limit his own freedom.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.150
In the same way, gym members tie themselves
to a financial commitment to prevent their

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:02.060
future selves from succumbing to the temptation
of not working out.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.610
Essentially, precommitment is an admission
of weakness.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.660
So, does it work?

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.009
Sometimes, yes.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.250
Setting up an automatic withdrawal on your
paycheck into an investment account is a good

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.990
way of precommitting to a savings plan.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.390
And buying healthy food at the grocery store
does precommit you to cooking at home rather

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.410
than eating out.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.150
But when it comes to gym memberships… the
results are a little less optimistic.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.960
According to a survey of over 5,000 American
gym members, 82% go less than once a week,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.530
and 22% stop going completely after 6 months.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.140
So much for precommitment.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.560
In light of those odds, maybe it’s best
to go with the monthly contract.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.990
Sure, it costs a bit more, but at least if
my future self betrays me I can drop out and

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.440
cut my losses.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.440
Right?

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.440
Maybe not.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.990
Another study on the habits of gym-goers (or,
non-gym-goers) found that members who subscribed

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:59.850
to a $70 monthly program ended up using it
only 4.3 times per month.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.421
Even though their gyms had a $10 per visit
option, they were essentially paying $17 per

00:03:04.421 --> 00:03:05.421
visit!

00:03:05.421 --> 00:03:09.319
And here’s where it gets weird: the whole
point of these monthly plans is that you can

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:10.800
cancel whenever you want.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.420
But people in the monthly plans were actually
17% more likely than annual members to keep

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.960
their subscriptions going after 12 months…
even if they weren’t using them!

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.460
Future Self, what the heck is wrong with you?

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.130
The answer is that monthly subscribers were
on an “auto-renewal” program, which simply

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:36.200
charged their credit cards every month without
needing explicit permission from the customer.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.069
Because it’s a passive transaction, these
auto-renewals don’t cause much “negative

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:44.840
utility,” what economists call the emotional
pain of losing money.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.990
If those people had to actually hand over
70 bucks in cash on the first of every month,

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.340
a lot more of them probably would’ve cancelled
their memberships.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.599
Auto-renewals, or “evergreen clauses,”
are pretty ubiquitous these days, and they

00:03:57.599 --> 00:03:58.850
are convenient.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:03.170
Imagine having to manually renew every regular
service you use!

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:08.099
But some companies are less than forthright
about it, hiding the clause behind free trials

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.370
or making cancelling an overcomplicated process.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.990
You often won’t even be notified when your
subscription is renewed.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:16.940
They want you to forget about them.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.790
Gym memberships are especially tough to cancel,
because it feels like admitting defeat.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:26.750
And many gyms charge re-sign up fees that
make members just keep paying in the hopes

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.020
that maybe they’ll start using it again.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.720
It sounds like a broken system, but this is
actually how it’s designed to function.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.500
See, running a gym is actually really expensive.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.780
High utility bills, big spaces filled with
tons of fancy equipment, a large staff of

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.800
trained professionals… in order to pay for
all this, they have to sign up way more people

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.060
than the facility can actually serve.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.410
Major fitness chains commonly recruit between
5 to 10 thousand members per location… which

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.070
have max capacities of around 3 to 5 hundred
people.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.040
If just 5% of their members showed up at the
same time, they’d have to start turning

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.240
people away.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.190
So despite all those motivational posters
and slogans, they’re counting on the vast

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:13.130
majority of their members to rarely (or never)
use the service they pay for.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.600
The silver lining is that if you happen to
be one of the small minority who actually

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.900
uses your gym regularly, well, you’re makin’
out like a bandit.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:26.690
3 or 4 dollars a visit is an incredible steal,
only made possible by all those other resolution-breakers

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.070
who are subsidizing your wellness.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:33.430
Now, of course we’re not saying that you
shouldn’t try to get into shape!

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:38.419
Regular exercise is one of the best things
you can do for your body, mind, and pocketbook,

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:42.720
since many expensive medical problems can
be prevented with a healthy lifestyle.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.990
But there are cheaper ways to kickstart it.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:49.900
For instance, you can buy a nice pair of running
shoes or a bicycle and chart a course through

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.130
your neighborhood.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:55.700
Home fitness equipment like weights, bands,
even machines can get pretty pricey, but at

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.470
least once you own them, you don’t have
to keep paying every month.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:03.430
There are also lots of inexpensive apps and
online courses and free YouTube videos that

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.810
can help get you started.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:08.980
Many fitness experts will say that the most
important thing isn’t what equipment you

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.500
use, or how many calories you burn in a day,
but finding a way to make exercise a lifetime

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.500
habit.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.940
So how do you do that?

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.180
Don’t look at us, this is a financial show!

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:23.700
But, we can say that making big financial
commitments doesn’t seem to work.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:29.020
If you already exercise three times a week,
joining a gym can be a great bargain.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.449
But if you’re just starting out, don’t
think you can extort your future self into

00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:36.140
exercising with the threat of a deadweight
loss.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:41.930
Turns out our future selves are pretty crafty
at wriggling out of precommitments.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:43.900
Especially when the pain of losing money 
is

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:48.960
no match for the pain I’m gonna feel in
my legs tomorrow.

